\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Where this leaves the computation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 The ILP does hit a wall: solve time grows fast in n (roughly 0s \uc0\u8594  90s \uc0\u8594  180s+, unresolved by n\uc0\u8776 18\'9624 for both widths). But that wall does not block computing the values. The macro-column dynamic program (precompute the column-to-column transitions between fringe states once, then one shortest-path sweep over n) gives M(w,n) exactly for every n up to 120 in under a second for widths 5 and 7. For the disputed and open cases it was cross-checked by a second, independently written cell-by-cell dynamic program, and it agrees with every ILP-certified entry above. The ILP's role is now an independent check on small n.\
What remains open is a proof, not a computation: that the generic residue patterns above hold for all n, not just n \uc0\u8804  120. Agreement up to 120 is strong evidence but not a proof. Two routes: the boundary-matching / cylinder construction in the style Hochberg uses for widths 13 and 15 (build a periodic fragment, match boundaries across the residue classes, and extend for all n past some threshold), or an argument from the fringe graph itself, since M(w,n) is a shortest-path value over a fixed finite graph; a proven bound on when such sequences become periodic would turn the computation up to that bound into a proof.\
